26-Oct-2026<br /><br />Dear Dr. Chang:<br /><br />I am writing in regards t=
o manuscript ID TCAS-II-28553-2026 entitled "HUANGDI: A Heterogeneous Macro=
 Computing-In-Memory Accelerator With Dual-Lane Stage Fusion for CNNs and V=
ision Transformers" which you submitted to the IEEE Transactions on Circuit=
s and Systems II: Express Briefs.<br /><br />Your manuscript has been revie=
wed by several experts in the field. Based on the recommendations given by =
the associate editor and in view of the comments and concerns expressed by =
reviewers found at the bottom of this letter, I regret to inform you that y=
our manuscript is REJECTED for publication in the IEEE Transactions on Circ=
uits and Systems II: Express Briefs.<br /><br />Thank you for considering t=
he IEEE Transactions on Circuits and Systems II: Express Briefs for the pub=
lication of your research. =C2=A0I hope the outcome of this specific submis=
sion will not discourage you from your submission of future manuscripts.<br=
 /><br />Sincerely,<br /><br />Prof. Edoardo Bonizzoni<br />Editor-in-Chief=
, IEEE Transactions on Circuits and Systems II: Express Briefs<br />edoardo=
.bonizzoni@unipv.it<br /><br /><br />Editors' Comments to the Authors:<br /=
><br />Associate Editor: 1<br />Comments to the Author:<br />Unfortunately,=
 the reviewers raised several shared concerns.<br /><br />A more in-depth a=
nalysis of the design-space exploration (DSE) is required to assess the rob=
ustness and generalizability of the selected architecture. The main concern=
s relate to model coverage, the completeness of the cost model, and the cri=
teria used to select the final configuration.<br /><br />The comparisons in=
 Table I involve heterogeneous design technologies and implementation stage=
s, which limit the fairness of direct comparisons. In particular, the table=
 includes DRAM-based architectures and results measured on fabricated silic=
on, while the proposed design is SRAM-based and evaluated at the post-synth=
esis stage. These differences should be acknowledged when interpreting the =
reported performance metrics.<br /><br />The reviewers also requested furth=
er implementation details, including the scheduler and mapping mechanisms, =
dataflow execution, and the roles of specific architectural components.<br =
/><br />The impact of key design choices on computational accuracy and end-=
to-end prediction quality is not adequately analyzed. In particular, the ac=
curacy degradation due to quantization and blockwise RMSNorm is not isolate=
d, and the numerical errors introduced by the proposed vector processing te=
chniques are not characterized.<br /><br />The evaluation does not isolate =
the benefits and overheads of individual architectural choices.<br /><br />=
Finally, the manuscript does not clearly distinguish its novel contribution=
s from known optimization techniques in neural-network accelerator design. =
Many proposed mechanisms, including pipelining and stage fusion, have been =
explored in prior work. Moreover, the proposed DSE methodology is not chara=
cterized against existing approaches in terms of novelty and benefits.<br /=
><br />Senior Area Editor: 2<br />Comments to Author:<br />(There are no co=
mments. Please check to see if comments were included as a file attachment =
with this e-mail or as an attachment in your Author Center.)<br /><br /><br=
 />Reviewers' Comments to the Authors:<br /><br />Reviewer: 1<br /><br />Co=
mments to the Author<br />This paper presents a design space exploration (D=
SE) of compute-in-memory (CIM) macros and proposes an architecture for acce=
lerating CNNs and Transformers. Although the work is interesting, several m=
ajor concerns need to be addressed:<br /><br />1. The paper proposes a Vect=
or Processing Unit (VPU) for implementing functions such as Softmax and RMS=
Norm. What error does the VPU introduce when computing these functions, and=
 how does its accuracy compare with that of other implementations?<br /><br=
 />2. What does =E2=80=9CN=E2=80=9D represent in Fig. 4? Is it a buffer or =
a more complex routing component? Since it supports data movement in multip=
le directions, its functionality and implementation should be clarified.<br=
 /><br />3. Table I compares CIM implementations based on different memory =
technologies, including SRAM and DRAM. These technologies have different ch=
aracteristics that affect the reported metrics. Which memory technology doe=
s the proposed architecture use, and how are these differences accounted fo=
r to ensure a fair comparison?<br /><br />4. How does the proposed DSE meth=
odology compare with existing DSE approaches? The paper does not provide a =
detailed comparison with prior DSE methodologies, making it difficult to as=
sess the advantages of the proposed approach.<br /><br />Reviewer: 2<br /><=
br />Comments to the Author<br />The manuscript presents HUANGDI, a heterog=
eneous digital SRAM CIM accelerator for unified CNN and vision Transformer =
acceleration. The architecture combines compiler-guided exploration of hete=
rogeneous CIM macro granularity, a dual-lane multicore dataflow supporting =
CNN inter-layer fusion and overlapping Transformer operations, and a shared=
 vector processing unit for online softmax and blockwise RMSNorm.<br /><br =
/>Overall, I find the work interesting and relevant to TCAS-II. The propose=
d architecture addresses important bottlenecks in unified CNN/Transformer a=
cceleration, and the combination of design-space exploration, architectural=
 design, hardware synthesis, and accuracy evaluation is appreciated. The re=
ported improvements in end-to-end execution cycles and global-buffer access=
es are promising.<br /><br />However, I have several concerns regarding the=
 generalizability of the design-space exploration, the novelty of the propo=
sed dataflow and vector processing techniques, and the completeness and fai=
rness of the hardware evaluation. The following points should be addressed =
to better substantiate the claimed contributions.<br /><br />1-Generalizabi=
lity of the heterogeneous CIM macro exploration:<br />Section II presents a=
 compiler-characterization-guided DSE that selects the heterogeneous 64+512=
 row CIM tile using ResNet-18 and ViT-B/16.<br /><br />Although the methodo=
logy is interesting, using only two networks to determine the macro configu=
ration raises concerns about its generalizability. Different networks, part=
icularly larger variants, may exhibit different reduction dimensions and ma=
pping requirements. Would the same heterogeneous configuration remain optim=
al, or would the DSE select another configuration requiring hardware redesi=
gn?<br /><br />In particular, VGG-16 and ViT-L/16 are already included in t=
he dataflow evaluation in Fig. 7 but are not considered in the macro-select=
ion process. Could the authors explain this choice?<br />I suggest extendin=
g the DSE evaluation to additional representative workloads or providing a =
sensitivity analysis showing how close the selected 64+512 configuration re=
mains to the workload-specific optimum. This would substantially strengthen=
 the first contribution and demonstrate that the proposed heterogeneous mac=
ro organization is not primarily optimized for the two selected networks.<b=
r /><br />2-Completeness of the DSE cost model and architectural parameter =
selection:<br />The two level DSE in Section II considers macro VMM and par=
tial-sum accumulation energy but explicitly excludes weight loading, contro=
l and interconnect.<br />Although the authors acknowledge this limitation, =
these contributions may be significant when comparing configurations with d=
ifferent macro sizes, numbers of macros, and communication requirements. Th=
eir exclusion could potentially affect the relative ranking of candidate ar=
chitectures.<br />Have the authors estimated the energy and area costs of t=
hese components? How sensitive is the selected configuration to their exclu=
sion? Even an approximate analysis or sensitivity study would help establis=
h the robustness of the proposed exploration methodology.<br /><br />Furthe=
rmore, while the selection of the 64 + 512 row macro combination is explain=
ed, it is less clear how the other architectural parameters were determined=
. In particular, why were four CIM tiles per core and four CIM cores select=
ed? Were these parameters fixed before the exploration, or were they jointl=
y optimized?<br />Since these architectural choices may influence utilizati=
on, communication overhead, and the optimal macro configuration, their sele=
ction should be justified.<br /><br />3-Novelty of the dual-lane multicore =
dataflow:<br />The manuscript proposes a dual-lane multicore dataflow suppo=
rting independent execution, cross-core accumulation, CNN inter-layer fusio=
n, and overlapping projection with self-attention across consecutive Transf=
ormer heads.<br />While these techniques can improve execution efficiency, =
inter-layer fusion, pipelining, and flexible mapping of CNN and Transformer=
 operations have been extensively explored in previous accelerators.<br />I=
n particular, references [12] and [13] already investigate unified CNN/Tran=
sformer acceleration through reconfigurable architectures and flexible data=
flows. Other heterogeneous CIM-based architectures, such as "EAGLE: A Flexi=
ble Heterogeneous Analog Compute-In-Memory Architecture With RISC-V Program=
mable Multi-Core Accelerators", have also investigated fine-grained inter-l=
ayer pipelining (including overlap across attention heads and projection la=
yers).<br />I would encourage the authors to clarify what specifically dist=
inguishes the proposed dual-lane dataflow from existing approaches.<br /><b=
r />In addition, Figure 5 illustrates the proposed execution modes, and Fig=
. 7 provides a useful ablation against single-lane and stage-separated conf=
igurations. However, the novelty would be more convincing if the authors ex=
plicitly differentiated their approach from conventional pipelining or doub=
le-buffered execution and clarified which architectural mechanism enables t=
he reported improvements.<br /><br />4-Blockwise RMSNorm:<br />According to=
 Equation (4), a fixed-size statistics block of 64 elements is used to esti=
mate a single RMS normalization factor, which is then applied to all data b=
locks of the input vector. This differs from conventional RMSNorm, where th=
e normalization factor is computed over the complete normalization dimensio=
n. How is the statistics block selected?<br /><br />Additionally, the formu=
lation includes a learned scaling vector. Are the original pretrained param=
eters reused, or is retraining or fine-tuning required? This should be expl=
icitly clarified.<br /><br />5- Comparison with prior accelerators:<br />th=
e proposed accelerator is evaluated through synthesis in 22-nm CMOS technol=
ogy, whereas Table I includes accelerators evaluated using different implem=
entation methodologies, including fabricated silicon measurements (e.g., [4=
]).<br />Although synthesis-based evaluation is valuable, comparisons betwe=
en pre-layout estimates and measured silicon results should be clearly qual=
ified. I suggest adding an implementation-status row to Table I distinguish=
ing synthesis, post-layout, and measured silicon results.<br /><br />Overal=
l assessment<br />The manuscript presents a relevant and potentially useful=
 approach to unified CNN and Transformer acceleration. I appreciate the eff=
ort to integrate heterogeneous CIM macro granularity, flexible multicore da=
taflow, and shared vector processing within a common accelerator, supported=
 by design-space exploration, synthesis, and accuracy evaluation.<br />Neve=
rtheless, the central contributions would benefit from stronger evidence of=
 generalizability, clearer differentiation from existing architectural and =
scheduling techniques, and a more complete assessment of the hardware costs=
 and evaluation baselines.<br />Addressing these points would significantly=
 improve the technical clarity and strengthen the claimed contributions of =
the manuscript.<br /><br />Reviewer: 3<br /><br />Comments to the Author<br=
 />This paper proposes HUANGDI, a heterogeneous digital SRAM compute-in-mem=
ory (CIM) accelerator for unified CNN and Vision Transformer acceleration. =
The design employs compiler-guided design-space exploration (DSE) to select=
 a 64-row + 512-row heterogeneous CIM tile, a dual-lane fusible dataflow to=
 improve core utilization and reduce intermediate data movement, and a UniS=
tream-VPU that shares hardware resources between online softmax and blockwi=
se RMSNorm. Synthesized in 22-nm CMOS technology, the proposed accelerator =
achieves energy efficiencies of 17.4 TOPS/W for ResNet-18 and 16.2 TOPS/W f=
or ViT-B/16. However, several issues and requests for clarification remain:=
<br />Major comments<br />=E2=80=A2 The paper motivates the proposed archit=
ecture by highlighting the challenges of unified CNN and ViT acceleration. =
However, the three bottlenecks identified in Fig. 1 can also arise in dedic=
ated CNN or ViT accelerators, rather than being specific to unified acceler=
ation. Please clarify how these challenges are exacerbated by supporting bo=
th workloads on a shared architecture and further justify the benefits of t=
he proposed unified design.<br />=E2=80=A2 Fig. 1(c) includes a MAC operati=
on followed by normalization in the softmax processing path, which appears =
to include the subsequent PV operation rather than softmax alone. Please cl=
arify whether the diagram represents only softmax or also includes the atte=
ntion computation, and revise the labeling accordingly.<br />=E2=80=A2 Sect=
ion II introduces compiler-guided DSE to select the 64-row + 512-row hetero=
geneous CIM tile. However, the DSE process is not sufficiently explained to=
 justify the selection of this specific macro combination. The authors shou=
ld provide a more detailed explanation of the DSE process and the final con=
figuration selection criteria.<br />=E2=80=A2 Fig. 6(b) proposes a shared d=
atapath for online softmax and blockwise RMSNorm, but the area and power be=
nefits of hardware resource sharing are not quantitatively evaluated. Pleas=
e provide an area and power comparison with separate datapath implementatio=
ns to demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed hardware sharing.<br />=
=E2=80=A2 Fig. 9 presents the power and area breakdown of the proposed acce=
lerator, but it does not quantify the additional area and power overhead in=
troduced by the proposed architecture. Please provide an area and power com=
parison with a baseline architecture without the dual-lane and fusion mecha=
nisms to clarify the additional hardware cost.<br />Minor comments<br />=E2=
=80=A2 The publication year of Reference [13] should be corrected from 2024=
 to 2025, consistent with the cited journal volume and issue.<br /><br /><b=
r />Reviewer: 4<br /><br />Comments to the Author<br />The paper presents a=
 unified digital SRAM-CIM accelerator, HUANGDI, that combines heterogeneous=
 CIM macros (64-row + 512-row), dual-lane stage fusion, and a shared stream=
ing vector processor (UniStream-VPU) for CNN and Vision Transformer acceler=
ation. However, the manuscript does not clearly establish the novelty beyon=
d prior work, justify several key architectural decisions, or isolate the c=
ontribution of the proposed techniques through strong baseline comparisons =
and analysis.<br /><br />1. Novelty is unclear<br />=E2=80=A2 What is the a=
ctual architectural novelty beyond combining heterogeneous macros, stage fu=
sion, and streaming vector processing? Which component is new, and which co=
mponents are adaptations of prior work?<br />=E2=80=A2 If each idea exists =
individually in prior literature, what capability does HUANGDI uniquely pro=
vide?<br />2. Baselines are favorable to the proposed design<br />=E2=80=A2=
 Why are comparisons primarily against decomposed baselines (single-lane, h=
omogeneous, stage-separated, conventional vector processing)?<br />=E2=80=
=A2 Why not compare against a strong integrated baseline combining existing=
 heterogeneous-CIM, fusion, and streaming approaches? How much benefit rema=
ins after introducing the same prior-art techniques into the baseline?<br /=
>=E2=80=A2 When reporting end-to-end cycle reductions, do the results inclu=
de weight loading/programming, routing, and data-movement overheads, or are=
 they limited to compute execution?<br /><br />3. The heterogeneous tile se=
lection is not fully convincing<br />=E2=80=A2 Why is the DSE performed pri=
marily using ResNet-18 and ViT-B/16? Would 64+512 still be selected for oth=
er CNNs and Transformers?<br />=E2=80=A2 Why are candidates restricted to a=
t most two macro sizes? Were configurations such as 64+256+512 evaluated?<b=
r />=E2=80=A2 The DSE excludes weight loading, interconnect, and control co=
sts. Why should the selected tile remain optimal once those costs are inclu=
ded?<br />4. Scheduler and mapping framework are under-described<br />=E2=
=80=A2 Many of the reported gains appear to rely on compiler-guided mapping=
 and scheduling, yet the scheduler is largely treated as a black box. What =
exactly is being optimized, and how much do the results depend on schedulin=
g quality?<br />=E2=80=A2 Key concepts such as "legal schedules" and "reduc=
tion waves" are introduced without sufficient explanation. What exactly con=
stitutes a "legal schedule"?<br /><br />5. Accuracy degradation is not anal=
yzed<br />=E2=80=A2 ViT-B/16 loses 3.8% Top-1 accuracy relative to FP32. Wh=
at causes this degradation?<br />=E2=80=A2 How much comes from INT8 quantiz=
ation, and how much comes from the proposed blockwise RMSNorm formulation?<=
br /><br />6. Transformer fusion is claimed but not quantified<br />=E2=80=
=A2 The paper explicitly claims that projection of head N is overlapped wit=
h attention computation of head N=E2=88=921 using the 512-row and 64-row ma=
cros. However, it is unclear what stages are included in the overlap (proje=
ction compute only vs. weight setup, data movement, replay routing, etc.).<=
br />=E2=80=A2 How much of the reported cycle reduction is directly attribu=
table to this overlap mechanism?<br /><br />7. Generality claims are strong=
er than the evidence<br />=E2=80=A2 The evaluation is largely limited to Re=
sNet-18 and ViT-B/16. How does the architecture behave for other transforme=
r variants or modern CNN families? Does the reported benefit persist across=
 different model sizes and attention structures?<br />8. Role of the Scale =
Fusion Unit is unclear<br />=E2=80=A2 The role of the Scale Fusion Unit is =
not sufficiently explained. Figure 4 introduces a dedicated MAC array outsi=
de the CIM cores, but the manuscript does not clearly describe which operat=
ions are mapped to this unit, why they cannot be handled by the accumulator=
 or UniStream-VPU.<br />=E2=80=A2 Is the MAC array used for residual additi=
on, attention output accumulation/rescaling, quantization scaling, or all o=
f the above? How frequently is it exercised by the evaluated workloads?<br =
/>=E2=80=A2 If the Scale Fusion Unit participates in the Transformer fusion=
 path, does it become part of the critical path during attention execution?=
 Are its latency and energy costs included in the reported results?
